-# indian_trading
+# Quant India
+
+Quant India is a Python 3.12 repository for research, portfolio, risk, execution,
+and operational workflows. This commit establishes the production repository
+foundation only; trading logic and external integrations are intentionally not
+implemented.
+
+## Safety boundaries
+
+The architecture is designed around the following non-negotiable boundaries:
+
+- No Market or IOC orders are created by this repository.
+- Any future execution request must pass through the risk engine before it can
+  reach an execution adapter.
+- Credentials and environment-specific values are supplied outside source
+  control.
+- Raw data, features, snapshots, and generated reports are local or externally
+  managed artifacts, not Git-managed datasets.
+
+These boundaries are documented now so that later modules can be implemented
+without weakening the operating model.
+
+## Requirements
+
+- Python 3.12
+- `uv` (preferred) or the standard-library `venv` module
+- GNU Make for the convenience commands in `Makefile`
+
+## Getting started
+
+### With `uv` (preferred)
+
+```bash
+uv venv --python 3.12
+source .venv/bin/activate
+uv pip install --requirement requirements/dev.txt
+uv pip install --editable .
+cp .env.example .env
+```
+
+### With `venv` and `pip`
+
+```bash
+python3.12 -m venv .venv
+source .venv/bin/activate
+python -m pip install --upgrade pip
+python -m pip install --requirement requirements/dev.txt
+python -m pip install --editable .
+cp .env.example .env
+```
+
+The equivalent Make target is `make install`. Set `PYTHON` if Python 3.12 is
+not the default executable, for example `make PYTHON=python3.12 install`.
+
+## Development commands
+
+Run these commands from an activated environment, or use the Make targets after
+`make install`:
+
+```bash
+make lint          # Ruff lint checks
+make format        # Apply Ruff formatting
+make format-check  # Verify formatting without changing files
+make test          # Run pytest
+make check         # Lint, formatting check, and tests
+make pre-commit    # Run all configured pre-commit hooks
+```
+
+The installed console entry point is `quant-india`. It is currently a safe
+no-op because application workflows have not been implemented yet.
+
+## Repository layout
+
+```text
+quant-india/
+├── config/
+├── data/
+│   ├── raw/
+│   ├── clean/
+│   ├── features/
+│   └── snapshots/
+├── ingestion/
+├── research/
+├── models/
+├── backtest/
+├── portfolio/
+├── agents/
+├── risk_kill/
+├── execution/
+├── reconciliation/
+├── orchestration/
+├── observability/
+├── dashboard/
+├── reports/
+├── tests/
+├── scripts/
+├── migrations/
+├── docs/
+├── .github/workflows/
+├── requirements/
+└── main.py
+```
+
+Each Python boundary has a module README and package docstring. See
+[`docs/architecture.md`](docs/architecture.md) for ownership and safety
+invariants, and [`docs/development.md`](docs/development.md) for contribution
+conventions.
+
+## Continuous integration
+
+GitHub Actions runs on pushes and pull requests. The workflow uses Python 3.12,
+installs the development requirements and editable project, then runs Ruff
+linting, Ruff formatting verification, and the complete pytest suite. Any failed
+step fails the workflow.
+
+## Dependency ownership
+
+Runtime requirements are intentionally empty in this foundation. The
+`requirements/` directory is split by module so future dependencies can be
+owned, reviewed, and removed without creating an unbounded global environment.
+Development tools are listed in `requirements/dev.txt` and mirrored in the
+`dev` optional dependency in `pyproject.toml` for `uv` and packaging workflows.
